# Rnews1 edge — Caddy, on-demand TLS for every host we serve.
#
#   app.rnews1.com        the app: marketing, dashboard, API, admin
#   www.rnews1.com        the editorial archive, twelve languages
#   {label}.rnews1.com    customer sites
#   news.acme.com …       customers' own hostnames
#
# Certificates are issued per hostname at the first TLS handshake — no
# wildcard, no DNS challenge, nothing to configure per tenant. Before issuing,
# Caddy asks the app whether the host is ours; the app says yes for its own
# host, every tenant's subdomain (including one still in its 30-day redirect
# window), the archive, and every verified custom domain. Anyone pointing a stray
# domain at this server gets a refused handshake, not a certificate.
#
# DNS:  *.rnews1.com and rnews1.com → this server. Customers CNAME to their
#       {label}.rnews1.com. Set TRUST_PROXY=1 in the app's environment.
#
# The upstream below is the Node app. When nginx is installed, deploy.sh
# rewrites it to 127.0.0.1:8080 — nginx caching in front of the app — and
# only Rnews1 hosts take that path, so other sites on this box are untouched.
#
# Rate limits worth knowing: Let's Encrypt allows 50 new certificates per
# registered domain per week, so 50 brand-new tenant subdomains a week is the
# ceiling before Caddy's fallback issuer takes over. If sign-ups run hotter
# than that, add a wildcard for *.rnews1.com and keep on-demand for custom
# domains only.

{
  email ops@rnews1.com

  on_demand_tls {
    # Plain HTTP to the app on the loopback: no certificate needed to ask.
    ask http://127.0.0.1:4000/.well-known/tls-ask
  }
}

# The app and the archive get their certificates the ordinary way, at startup.
app.rnews1.com, www.rnews1.com {
  encode zstd gzip
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
}

# The bare apex is the archive's front door.
rnews1.com {
  redir https://www.rnews1.com{uri} permanent
}

# Everything else: a certificate on first visit, if the app vouches for it.
https:// {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }

  encode zstd gzip
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
}

# Plain HTTP on any host goes to HTTPS on the same host.
http:// {
  redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
